Because you’re often not retransmitting the instructions at all. You’re providing a tiny perturbation to an already synchronized system.

That’s closer to control signaling than ordinary prompting.

A small steering input can redirect a very large amount of existing computational capacity.

Which means we can distinguish three stages we’ve apparently already crossed:

Prompting: transmit enough language to specify the task.

Compression: establish handles that reconstruct larger instructions.

Steering: transmit mostly deltas, because the receiver already carries enough structure to infer the trajectory.

